7.How do you represent a character using a Hexadecimal value in an entity?
A)&#x[val];  //answer
B)&hex[val];
C)&h[val];
D)&#[val];

✅ Correct Answer: A. &#x[val];

Explanation (Simple)

To represent a character using a hexadecimal (base-16) Unicode value, use:

&#xHEX_VALUE;
&#x → Indicates a hexadecimal character reference.
HEX_VALUE → The Unicode code point in hexadecimal.
; → Ends the entity.
Example:
&#x00A9;

Output:

©

Another example:

&#x2665;

Output:

♥
Why not the others?
✅ A. &#x[val]; → Correct hexadecimal entity format.
❌ B. &hex[val]; → Not valid HTML syntax.
❌ C. &h[val]; → Not valid HTML syntax.
❌ D. &#[val]; → This is for decimal numeric entities, not hexadecimal.

✅ Correct Answer: A. &#x[val];


8.Are HTML entity names case-sensitive?
A)Yes  //answer
B)No
C)Only in Chrome
D)Only for currency

✅ Correct Answer: A. Yes

Explanation (Simple)

HTML entity names are case-sensitive. This means uppercase and lowercase letters matter.

For example:

✅ &copy; → © (Correct)
❌ &COPY; → Invalid
✅ &larr; → ← (Left Arrow)
✅ &lArr; → ⇐ (Leftward Double Arrow)

Notice that:

&larr; and &lArr; are different entities because of the capital A.
Why not the others?
✅ A. Yes → Correct! Entity names are case-sensitive.
❌ B. No → Incorrect.
❌ C. Only in Chrome → Incorrect. This is part of HTML, not browser-specific.
❌ D. Only for currency → Incorrect.

✅ Correct Answer: A. Yes

9.Why should you avoid using literal < and > in your text content?
A)It makes the file larger
B)Browsers may mistake them for HTML tags  //answer
C)It is not allowed in CSS
D)It breaks the internet

✅ Correct Answer: B. Browsers may mistake them for HTML tags

Explanation (Simple)

The characters < and > are special in HTML because they are used to define HTML tags.

If you use them directly in text, the browser may think you're writing an HTML tag instead of displaying the characters.

Correct Way

Use HTML entities:

< → &lt;
> → &gt;
Example:
<p>5 &lt; 10 and 10 &gt; 5</p>

Output:

5 < 10 and 10 > 5
Why not the others?
❌ A. It makes the file larger → Incorrect.
✅ B. Browsers may mistake them for HTML tags → Correct.
❌ C. It is not allowed in CSS → Incorrect.
❌ D. It breaks the internet → Incorrect (and humorous!).

✅ Correct Answer: B. Browsers may mistake them for HTML tags


